WEBVTT

00:05.770 --> 00:11.500
Remember we learned before in the lessons on number sets that fractions are rational numbers, which

00:11.500 --> 00:14.200
means that they are the ratio of integers.

00:14.440 --> 00:21.460
If you take two integers, like three and four, let's say, and you divide one by the other, you get

00:21.460 --> 00:23.530
three fourths, a fraction.

00:23.650 --> 00:27.910
And we read that as three divided by four or three over four.

00:28.420 --> 00:32.890
Fractions are tough, but they're also one of the concepts from this course that you'll use most often

00:32.890 --> 00:33.880
in real life.

00:33.880 --> 00:38.740
And that's because fractions answer real world questions like What's a better value?

00:38.800 --> 00:45.100
£2 of oranges for $3 or four and one half pounds of oranges for $6.25.

00:45.880 --> 00:50.470
Later on in this lesson group, we'll work through all the rules for fraction arithmetic, but we'll

00:50.470 --> 00:55.720
start by looking at equivalent fractions so that we can understand that fractions like two thirds and

00:55.720 --> 01:01.600
ten over 15, while they may look different, actually represent the same value.
